def build(course, m):
    return course(91, 'Coastal Communities and Blue Economy Foundations',
        'Coastal Communities\nand Blue Economy\nFoundations',
        'Map how coastal activities, ecosystems and public decisions affect livelihoods, access and social wellbeing.', [
        m('Map coastal livelihoods and ecosystems',
            'Trace a coastal resource through work, household use and ecological dependencies.',
            """
A coastal economy includes work on water and on land. Follow a catch from preparation to landing, processing, transport, retail and household consumption. Other coastal activities may use the same beach, harbour or mangrove edge. One organisation can benefit from a new dock while people carrying fish through a landing area lose an established route. [S81]

People use coastal resources for food, earnings, care and cultural ties. Income recorded at first sale leaves out some home consumption and unpaid work. Basurto and colleagues distinguish several measures of fisheries contribution, using country case data and extrapolation to estimate different dimensions. The global estimates suggest domains to investigate. A village profile requires evidence from its own population and period. [R91]

Identify the ecological function that supports each specific activity. A sheltered creek may enable access to a landing site. A mangrove belt can support habitat and reduce some wave exposure under suitable conditions. Name what is observed and what requires environmental expertise. An asset can have several functions and those functions can operate over different seasons and locations. [S82]

Make a table with columns for activity, people, site, dependence, evidence and an uncertainty. Include one activity that lacks an official trade record. Suppose a new gate allows trucks in but blocks the walking route. Trace the likely change through processing time, local food access and earnings. Mark these effects as hypotheses and name the people whose experiences could help verify them. [S81] Consider how time spent queuing, carrying fish or finding another route changes a worker’s available hours and a household’s ability to obtain food.
            """,
            'A coastal redevelopment proposal records boat sales but leaves out processors, local buyers and households that consume part of the catch.',
            'Draw the value chain and identify a dependence, an excluded group and a missing measure at three points.',
            'Record household use and land-based work separately. Specify the habitat or route that sustains each activity and who can verify it.',
            """
Map work along the chain.|Where should gear repair appear in the map?|Before harvest, as an activity supporting production.|Only under household consumption.|Only under recorded exports.|Under coastal hazard assessment.|Preparation is part of the value chain.
Capture household use.|Which value is directly missed by a first-sale ledger?|Fish retained for household consumption.|The price of a recorded auction sale.|The volume sold at auction.|The date of the recorded transaction.|Household use can occur without a sale.
Identify ecosystem dependence.|Which entry links a coastal activity to a feature?|The sheltered creek provides access to the landing point.|The pier has a new sign.|The workers share a surname.|The report uses a colour scale.|A dependency explains how conditions make a task possible.
Use estimates at the right scale.|How should the global fisheries study inform a village assessment?|Suggest dimensions to measure and verify them locally.|Copy its global ratios as village counts.|Treat global totals as this season's observations.|Allocate the village share by population alone.|Data collection and extrapolation have a defined scale.
Trace access effects.|What is the first plausible effect of closing the walking gate?|Porters and buyers may lose their established passage.|Boat ownership automatically changes.|The mangrove belt disappears that day.|Every price rises by a known amount.|Start with the changed route and test downstream effects.
            """),
        m('Compare competing shoreline uses',
            'Compare shoreline options using the same access, ecological and social criteria.',
            """
Start comparison with the purpose of each use. A sheltered landing supports fish handling, a tourism walkway supports public visits, and a restoration zone supports habitat recovery. Map when and where each requires space. A conflict may come from a bottleneck during particular hours. Describe who needs continuous access and who can change the time or location affordably. [S81]

Treat formal permission, practical access and a claim about rights as distinct evidence. A map may omit seasonal paths or customary use. A person may value a route that they do not formally own. Document claims and their sources without deciding title in the classroom. Identify when a verified decision maker, consultation or specialist advice would be required before narrowing access. [S83]

Compare the options under common criteria. Include marine habitat, food availability, work and income, access for people with disabilities, distribution of costs and agency capacity to maintain a route. Check cumulative pressure from nearby development as well as the proposed project. A small additional change may matter at an already congested location. Flag the conditions on which your comparison depends. [S82] [S84]

If an alternative landing is available, ask whether it works at the right time and whether equipment, fees, safe access and transport are practical. Interview attendance in a proposed project would describe who attended. It could miss others with a similar claim and different information needs. Consider who bears the extra time if the route changes and how people can influence a revision. [R92] Use a time map covering ordinary work and peak landing periods. Explain where differences in timing matter most.
            """,
            'A promenade would cross the only morning unloading route. An alternative site is open later, charges an entry fee and has no covered unloading area.',
            'Compare a shared-time route and relocation using the same five criteria. Flag one claim requiring formal review.',
            'The later route transfers costs to workers. Check practical access at the relevant time and document which decision requires authority.',
            """
Map space and time together.|Which detail helps locate the real conflict?|The hours when unloading overlaps pedestrian demand.|Only the official name of the promenade.|The number of images in the proposal.|The height of the information board.|Time and location reveal specific overlap.
Distinguish claims and authority.|How should a seasonal access claim enter the classroom map?|Record the reported use and identify the authority that can assess it.|Declare immediate legal title from the claim.|Erase the route because it is seasonal.|Attribute it to all workers without checking.|A reported claim and an authorised determination have different status.
Use consistent criteria.|Which comparison treats alternatives fairly?|Apply the same criteria for access, workload and ecological effects.|Count fees for only the shared option.|Consider connectivity in only one season for all options.|Compare revenue for one route and attendance for the other.|Common criteria make trade-offs interpretable.
Check cumulative effects.|What should a team consider beyond the promenade footprint?|Whether other changes already narrow usable access nearby.|Only its colour plan.|Only work inside the boundary.|Only the new operator's balance sheet.|Multiple changes can combine at a shared bottleneck.
Test a practical alternative.|Which fact most directly challenges the replacement site?|Its gates open after the regular unloading window.|It has a newer logo.|Its new sign has a different title.|It lies within the same district.|A replacement route must function when people need it.
            """),
        m('Define a social work response',
            'Specify a proportionate response with service, advocacy and specialist referral roles.',
            """
A practitioner can help people describe a change in daily life, organise safe access to services and bring unresolved concerns to an appropriate decision forum. Begin with the practical effect on someone such as a processor losing a sheltered place to work. Ask what help is wanted and whether others face similar barriers. Protect confidentiality when combining individual accounts into a shared issue. [S81]

Environmental pressures can overlap. An IJSDS article on Emohua uses existing sources to discuss degradation, livelihood loss and advocacy in an oil-producing area. It offers reasons to ask about environmental and social service responsibilities. The paper's locality and descriptive design limit inference about which source caused a specific incident or whether a particular intervention improved outcomes. [S85]

Distinguish immediate service needs from longer-term rights and policy questions. Practical support may include connecting a household to an authorised service or arranging an accessible meeting. A technical environmental claim may require a qualified assessment. A dispute about land or compensation should reach a role with recognised authority. Explain which evidence you hold and record questions that remain open. [S82]

Draft an action record that names a stated concern, its evidence, the person or group affected, an immediate service step and a responsible role. Include a date to review what actually happened. A referral sent does not guarantee a service received. Track receipt and acknowledge delays. When discussing a shared issue, name the source category and avoid identifying details that could expose individuals. [S81] Record which actions the practitioner can take within the agency and which depend on a separate decision.
            """,
            'A processor asks for service support after access to the beach changes. A village notice attributes the change to an unverified environmental event.',
            'Separate the support request from attribution. Name the immediate referral, authorised technical route and a review date.',
            'Address the stated need first. Record attribution as unresolved and refer the evidence question to a competent role.',
            """
Start with the person's stated concern.|Which first step respects the processor's priorities?|Ask what access or service support they need now.|Announce the cause before listening.|Promise payment on behalf of a regulator.|Collect public signatures naming the processor.|A proportionate response follows the expressed need.
Use IJSDS within its method.|What can the Emohua discussion contribute to the scenario?|Questions about linked livelihood and service pressures.|Proof of what caused this beach change.|A measured impact of a new service.|A universal ranking of remedies.|A secondary thematic account frames local inquiry.
Refer specialist decisions.|Who assesses competing technical explanations of a real environmental incident?|The competent environmental authority or specialist.|A learner acting alone.|The first person to post a notice.|The finance sponsor without evidence.|Technical attribution requires evidence and appropriate competence.
Protect identifying details.|What can be shared in an authorised summary?|An aggregate pattern with details limited to the decision's need.|A client list for unrestricted circulation.|The address of the first complainant by default.|All case notes copied verbatim.|Purpose and privacy guide aggregation.
Track service receipt.|Which marker shows a useful follow-up step?|The receiving role acknowledges the referral and reports the next action.|The sender adds a tick without checking.|The forum takes a group photo.|The concern is marked resolved after posting.|Acknowledgement and delivery are distinct events.
            """),
    ])
